WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.940
- Today, we're talking about Maritime Law,

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.090
which is why I'm standing next

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.350
to my yacht, the Legal Seagull.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.973
Wait, where did it go?

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.200
Damn you, pirates.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.690
(trumpet sounds)

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.550
Ahoy, legal eagles, today,
we're talking about pirates,

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:18.830
specifically sea pirates
and the Law of the Sea,

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.100
because believe it or not,

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.510
pirates actually had a huge effect on,

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:24.490
not only the founding of our country,

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.970
but also the way that our laws
were shaped both domestically

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.350
and internationally.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.180
So let's talk about what
it means to be a pirate

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.410
and why that's incredibly illegal.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.780
So first we need to talk about
what laws govern the ocean.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.240
And there's actually three
overlapping types of law.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.240
First there's Maritime Law,

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.030
which is generally the law
related to private ships

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:45.300
and commercial shipping.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.390
And then there's Admiralty Law,

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:49.030
which generally relates
to the law of shipping,

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:51.110
whether it's navigable waters in rivers

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:52.810
or on the ocean itself.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:53.810
And then there's the Law of the Sea,

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.060
which is an actual thing,

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.210
that is the international law related

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.610
to the use of oceans and seas.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.930
So Maritime and Admiralty
Law tends to be domestic,

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.470
while the Law of the Sea tends

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.010
to be the international rules
amongst different nations.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:09.190
Now, shipping was incredibly important

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:11.000
to the United States
when it was a new nation.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.030
And that's why it's
mentioned several times

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:14.530
in the US Constitution.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.120
Article three defines the boundaries

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:17.820
of subject matter
jurisdiction for the courts.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.610
Specifically, it extends
to the judicial power

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.670
of the United States to
quote "all Cases of Admiralty

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.000
and maritime Jurisdiction."

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.110
And if you're a US lawyer
dealing with Maritime Law,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.050
you're probably dealing

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.900
with laws like the Merchant
Marine Act of 1928,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.766
also known as the Jones Act.

00:01:30.766 --> 00:01:32.720
This act gives sailors the right

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.290
to sue negligent sea
captains and companies,

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:36.640
if they're injured on the seas.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.860
And it also highly
restricts the type of ships

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.080
that are allowed to transport
goods in the United States.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.890
Specifically, the Jones
Act says that any ship

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:47.310
that transports goods between
two different US states has

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.950
to be built in the United States,

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.740
be owned 75% by US citizens,

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.070
be crewed at least 75% by US
citizens and fly a US flag.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.330
Now you're probably thinking who cares.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.432
This is just an esoteric law about ships,

00:01:59.432 --> 00:02:02.980
but it actually has a huge
impact on the US economy.

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.770
And for you, my fellow US consumer,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.780
can make things incredibly expensive.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.820
For example, the cost of
shipping one container

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.282
from Los Angeles to
Shanghai is about $900.

00:02:12.282 --> 00:02:14.490
Whereas the cost of
shipping that same container

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:18.490
from Los Angeles to
Hawaii is about $10,000.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:21.180
And as a result, states
like Alaska and Hawaii

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.502
and territories like Puerto Rico

00:02:22.502 --> 00:02:25.900
have incredibly high shipping
rates just to get things

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.190
from one state to another.

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:31.000
Whereas those rates often don't
apply to foreign countries.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.210
And while you might be thinking, Hey,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.260
it's good for American
shipbuilders and American sailors.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:37.890
Well, there aren't actually that many.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.870
US shipbuilding basically
doesn't exist anymore,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.150
outside of the defense industry,

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.500
there are less than a hundred ships

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.430
that meet the requirements
of the Jones Act.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:46.954
So in times of emergency,

00:02:46.954 --> 00:02:50.260
sometimes these states are
just completely screwed.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.950
This might be the
dumbest law on the books.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.330
It's enough to drive you to piracy,

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:56.880
which is what we're
going to talk about next.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.030
So it might not surprise you to learn

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:01.050
that if you commit the act
of piracy on the high seas,

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.778
you can be prosecuted as such

00:03:02.778 --> 00:03:05.610
and during the golden age of piracy,

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.160
there were thousands and
thousands of pirates operating

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.260
in the Caribbean and
off the Barbary Coast,

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.980
which had a huge effect on
the formation of this nation.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.160
In the Caribbean, they boarded
merchant ships from Britain,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:16.600
the Netherlands, France and Portugal,

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.380
and played big powers against each other.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:19.960
Some of their exploits are legendary.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.780
Blackbeard captured a former
slave ship named the Concorde,

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.010
renamed it Queen Anne's Revenge

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.630
and blockaded Charleston Harbor in 1718.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.650
Piracy was such a menace
that it made its way

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:31.060
into the constitution.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.820
Article one, section eight, clause 10,

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.147
gives Congress the power to quote,

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.080
"define and punish piracy and felonies

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.196
on the high seas and offenses
against the law of nations."

00:03:39.196 --> 00:03:42.450
And in 1790 Congress
used that authorization

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.060
to create the first anti-piracy act.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.490
And this had nothing to do with software,

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.060
it was about actual sea pirates.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.210
The statute defined piracy
as murder or robbery

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:52.940
on the high seas or any
other crime committed

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.490
on the high seas that would be punishable

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:56.830
by death under US law,
if committed on land.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:59.690
The Supreme Court held that
the 1790 statute was limited

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.970
in its scope and American
courts couldn't penalize people

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.070
who committed piracy,

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.636
if they weren't American citizens sailing

00:04:04.636 --> 00:04:06.360
on a US flag vessel,

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.370
or if they didn't inflict
actual harm on US citizens.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.670
Congress eventually
amended the law in 1819,

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.040
specifying that piracy
didn't need a specific nexus

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.220
to the United States.

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.130
That's actually very rare in law

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.520
because if you have watched
this channel before, you know

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:22.000
that standing is an incredibly
important requirement

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.240
for almost any US lawsuit or prosecution.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.250
However, rather than
defining what piracy meant,

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:28.690
as it did in the original statute,

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.970
it referred to the crime
of piracy as defined

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.400
by the law of nations.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.800
The punishment for piracy
under the 1890 law was death.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.349
In 1909, the penalty was
changed to life imprisonment

00:04:38.349 --> 00:04:41.290
and the 1909 law hasn't been
amended since its passage.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.280
And it's found at 18 USC 1651,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.730
but the first challenge of
the 1819 law came swiftly

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:47.950
in 1820.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:50.190
When a man named Smith
participated in a mutiny

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.980
on a private vessel
commissioned by the colony

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:54.090
of Buenos Aires.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.950
Smith and his mates
imprisoned the captain,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.640
seized another pirate ship
called the Irresistible,

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.260
and robbed a Spanish vessel.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.860
Smith was convicted to piracy,

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.580
but he took his conviction
to the Supreme Court.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:06.350
Smith argued that the definition
of piracy was too vague,

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:07.445
but the court disagreed.

00:05:07.445 --> 00:05:09.530
The Supreme Court held
that piracy was defined

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:11.390
with reasonable certainty
by the law of nations

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:15.270
and that robbery on the high
seas was a component of piracy.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.474
The nations hate piracy,
and they hate pirates,

00:05:17.474 --> 00:05:19.724
which is why they want
to prosecute pirates

00:05:19.724 --> 00:05:21.230
whenever they can.

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:24.400
So the US and many other
developed countries have taken

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.320
a universal approach to this kind of law.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.610
This kind of international
law is actually very rare,

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.210
but it did serve as a
template for the prosecutions

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.840
in Nuremberg and Tokyo
following World War II.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.680
Now it took 200 years for the
US to prosecute another case

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.270
of piracy, but in 2010,

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:42.290
the US actually prosecuted
two different cases of piracy.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.110
The first US versus Said involved pirates,

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.130
who shot at the USS Ashland,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:48.360
the pirates boarded a small
skiff and fired weapons

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.530
at the Ashland, but
didn't attempt to board.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:51.880
The pirates moved to dismiss the case,

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:54.530
since they didn't board or
take control of the USS Ashland

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:56.030
and the US District
Court granted the motion.

00:05:56.030 --> 00:05:56.863
The court said

00:05:56.863 --> 00:05:58.520
that the piracy statute
only allowed prosecutions

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.953
for quote "robbery or forcible
depredations upon the sea."

00:06:01.953 --> 00:06:03.720
The definition given to piracy

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.300
by the United States
Supreme Court in Smith,

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:06.990
190 years earlier.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.250
But in the sister case
in US versus Hassan,

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.410
the Fourth Circuit expanded
the definition of piracy

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:12.910
beyond just robbery.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.400
In that case, Somali pirates,

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.360
mistakenly thought a US military vessel,

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.955
the USS Nicholas was
actually a merchant ship.

00:06:18.955 --> 00:06:20.820
The pirates opened fire on the ship

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:22.860
and the USS Nicholas crew fired back.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:24.190
The pirates agreed to flee,

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:25.920
but they were captured
and brought to the US,

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.920
where they became the first
men convicted of piracy

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:29.066
in 200 years.

00:06:29.066 --> 00:06:30.790
They were given life sentences.

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.357
However, the men thought
that they could appeal based

00:06:32.357 --> 00:06:33.641
on the Said case.

00:06:33.641 --> 00:06:35.720
They had not boarded the USS Nicholas,

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:37.330
nor did they rob the vessel.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.260
Unfortunately for them,

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.340
the Fourth Circuit expanded
the definition of robbery

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.720
and upheld the convictions.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:45.510
The court held that when
Congress enacted 18 USC 1651,

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.320
and provided for piracy to be
defined by the law of nations,

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.330
Congress knew that the
definition of piracy would expand

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.850
and evolve as the law of nations evolved.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:55.680
The court noted that the
international definition

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.190
of piracy has included many actions

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:58.560
beyond robbery and murder.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.140
The Fourth Circuit held
that limiting the definition

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.253
of piracy to robbery on
the high seas would quote,

00:07:02.253 --> 00:07:05.010
"render it incongruous with
the modern law of nations

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:06.110
and prevent the federal courts

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:09.430
from exercising universal
jurisdiction in piracy cases."

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:10.870
The Fourth Circuit reversed the dismissal

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:12.550
of the piracy count in Said.

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:14.620
The bottom line is that
under modern US law,

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.140
piracy on the high seas
involves things other

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:18.560
than just robbery.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.760
So pirates, be prepared to walk the plank.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:23.929
And at this point we're pretty
good at prosecuting pirates.

00:07:23.929 --> 00:07:28.420
America kicking pirates' ass
since 1801, with I guess,

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.620
a few exceptions to that rule.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.850
Unfortunately, we've
replaced pirates with trash.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:35.990
The oceans are absolutely
clogged with trash,

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:38.840
which is why it's time that
we all did something about it.

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:40.830
Which is why I joined TeamSeas,

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:43.620
a group of YouTube creators who are trying

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.902
to raise $30 million to remove
30 million pounds of trash

00:07:46.902 --> 00:07:48.620
from the ocean.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:49.860
Now, this is normally
the part of the video

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.080
where I would talk about
Curiosity Stream and Nebula,

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.210
but this is more important.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:54.820
And that's why Curiosity Stream decided

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.239
to donate their sponsor
time to allow me to tell you

00:07:57.239 --> 00:07:59.070
about TeamSeas.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:00.830
It couldn't be more simple.

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:03.380
There's a link on screen and
below this video right now,

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:05.470
that will take you to the
donation button for TeamSeas.

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.040
And if you could donate,
I'd really appreciate it.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:10.202
100% of the proceeds go to our partners,

00:08:10.202 --> 00:08:12.860
the Ocean Conservancy,
and the Ocean Cleanup.

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:13.693
And it couldn't be easier.

00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:15.460
The link below we'll take
you to the donation page

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.867
for TeamSeas and no amount is too small.

00:08:17.867 --> 00:08:20.060
$1 means one less pound of trash

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.467
that makes it into the ocean.

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:23.588
$5 means five pounds of trash and plastic

00:08:23.588 --> 00:08:25.480
that won't be in the ocean.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.030
Together, we can rid the ocean of plastic

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:28.740
and trash the same way
that we rid the ocean

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.123
of those dirty pirates.

00:08:30.123 --> 00:08:34.049
Now, if you'll, excuse me,
I'm going to go find my boat.

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.966
(piano music ends)

